We claim every pair of elements of $A$ must lie in $C$, so the answer is $\binom n2$. Indeed, if some pair $\{a,b\}
otin C$, label $a$ and $b$ both $n-1$ and the other $n-2$ elements $1,\ldots,n-2$; then no element of $C$ has both ends with the same label, so $C$ is $(n-1)$-separable. Conversely if $C$ is the whole $2$-completion, any labelling with fewer than $n$ labels repeats a label on two elements, and that pair lies in $C$, so $C$ is not $m$-separable for $m<n$.

**Answer: $\binom n2$**
